ENTRYPOINT vs CMD in Docker – Explained Simply

In every Dockerfile, we define what the container should do when it starts. For that, we use either CMD or ENTRYPOINT.
Both look similar… but behave very differently.

CMD – Default command, jo replace ho sakta hai
Socho CMD ek default instruction hai. If user runs the container without passing any command, CMD will execute.

But the moment user passes a command while running the container — Docker ignores CMD.
        CMD ["python3", "app.py"]
If you run:
       docker run myimage
It runs: python3 app.py

But if you run:
        docker run myimage ls -l`
Then it overrides CMD and runs: ls -l
Matlab CMD ka kaam hai "default dena", par user bolega to uska sunega.


ENTRYPOINT – Fixed command, jo hamesha chalega
ENTRYPOINT ek fixed rule jaisa hai — no matter what you pass while running the container, ENTRYPOINT always executes.
         ENTRYPOINT ["python3", "app.py"]
Now:
          docker run myimage
         → runs: python3 app.py

           docker run myimage test_arg
          → runs: python3 app.py test_arg
Yahan pe user ka argument append hota hai, but main command ENTRYPOINT hi rehta hai.
Combine ENTRYPOINT and CMD

Best practice hai: Dono ka use karo together.
            ENTRYPOINT ["python3"]
            CMD ["app.py"]
Then:
            docker run myimage
            → python3 app.py
 
             docker run myimage script.py
             → python3 script.py

ENTRYPOINT fix karta hai kya chalana hai, CMD batata hai default argument kya hona chahiye.




CMD is like: “Agar kuch nahi bola to yeh karlo.”
ENTRYPOINT is like: “Chahe kuch bhi bolo, yahi karna hai.”
CMD flexible hai. ENTRYPOINT strict hai.

Final Words
Docker CMD bolta hai: “Main default hoon.”
ENTRYPOINT bolta hai: “Main rule hoon.”


Interview me sirf syntax mat bolo — example do, real-world use batao.
Yahi difference sabse zyada confusion create karta hai — aur clarity yahi confidence laata hai.
